title: 'PROBLEM : links and content need similar treatment'
pid: b230848f-19d1-4e4c-8731-ab26b340c4b7
body: |2-
  Consider an atom A whose 'presents' section looks like this:
  - X
  - Y
  - Z
  -- Z' which Z presents
  --- Z'' which Z' presents
  -- W which Z does not present

  When A presents, it should present Z in a manner that indicates
  we are seeing "what A presents of Z".
  Note that what A presents of Z can include things that Z itself presents,
  as well as things it does not. The latter are like "what A says about Z".

  Z might be meaningless without A. For instance, an atom 'reasons to do it'
  is probably about its sole presenter.
  It presents no view of its own,
  but rather extends the view emanating from its presenter.

  If A presents Z in its entirety, A cannot present selectively from Z.
  So the natural way to do this is for A to contain not Z itself,
  but an atom that links to Z.
